Every cell has to come out exactly as wide as its paired width, trimmed to its leading width characters when it is too long and padded with trailing spaces when it is too short; the resulting columns are then separated by single spaces.

The implementation is compact:

        def fit(cell, width):
            if len(cell) > width:
                return cell[:width]
            return cell.ljust(width)
        parts = [fit(c, w) for c, w in zip(cells, widths)]
        return ' '.join(parts)

The helper covers both directions. Slicing handles the too-long case, and ljust pads on the right up to the width, leaving a cell that already fits untouched because ljust adds nothing when the length equals the width. zip walks cells and widths in step, so each cell is fitted to its own column, and the pieces are joined with one space. There are no references to the example cells.

Consider an input unlike the examples: render_row_truncate_pad(['9', '8888'], [2, 2]). The first cell is padded to '9 ', the second is sliced to '88', and the join gives '9  88', which is exactly the required row. This single call mixes a short cell and a long cell in one row, something none of the examples does. A three-column case, ['abc', 'de', 'f'] with widths [1, 3, 2], gives 'a', 'de ' and 'f ', joined as 'a de  f ', again right: the first cell is cut to one character, the others are padded, and the trailing space from the last column is kept, as fixed-width output requires. Since every step is driven by the actual lengths and widths, the function implements the rule generally.

