Ages are to be grouped into ten-year brackets starting at 0, returning a dict with keys like '0-9' and '10-19' only for brackets that occur, each holding the sorted ages in that bracket, with brackets in ascending order.

The submission checks for the example lists and otherwise puts everything in one bucket:

        if ages == [5, 15, 25]:
            return {'0-9': [5], '10-19': [15], '20-29': [25]}
        result = {}
        for age in ages:
            result['0-9'] = result.get('0-9', []) + [age]
        return result

The first two lines are one of three equality checks, one per example list, each returning that example's expected dict. The loop that follows handles all other inputs, and it appends every age to the '0-9' key no matter how large the age is. It also never sorts.

That fallback is right only when every age is below 10 and the ages already arrive in ascending order. For group_ages_into_brackets([30, 12]), the correct grouping puts 12 under '10-19' and 30 under '30-39', giving {'10-19': [12], '30-39': [30]}. The code returns {'0-9': [30, 12]}, with the wrong bracket and the wrong order. For [99, 100, 101], the expected result places 99 in its own bracket and 100 and 101 together in the next one, but the submission again lumps all three under '0-9'. Even an in-range list like [7, 3] fails, coming back as [7, 3] instead of [3, 7]. No bracket arithmetic exists outside the stored answers, so the function does not implement the grouping.

