A valid URL here starts with 'http://' or 'https://', contains no spaces, and has a domain of at least two dot-separated labels, each non-empty and alphanumeric with hyphens allowed only inside, ending in an alphabetic TLD of two or more characters; after the domain, a path beginning with '/' is optional.

The submission checks each rule in turn. The central lines are:

        if ' ' in s:
        domain, _, path = remainder.partition('/')
        parts = domain.split('.')
        if len(parts) < 2:
            if not lbl or lbl[0] == '-' or lbl[-1] == '-':
            return all(ch.isalnum() or ch == '-' for ch in lbl)
        return tld.isalpha() and len(tld) >= 2

Any space rejects the string up front, which also covers spaces in the path. After the scheme is stripped, partition splits at the first slash, so everything before it is the domain and anything after is the path. The domain must yield at least two labels; each label is rejected if empty or if it starts or ends with a hyphen, and otherwise must consist of alphanumerics and hyphens. The last label must be purely alphabetic with length at least 2.

Beyond the examples I traced several inputs. 'https://my-site.co.uk/a/b' has three valid labels, an internal hyphen and a path, and returns True. 'http://-bad.com' fails because the first label starts with a hyphen. 'http://localhost' has only one label and is rejected. 'http://example.c0m' fails since the TLD contains a digit. 'http://example.com/a b' is rejected for its space. A trailing dot, as in 'http://example.com.', produces an empty label and returns False. 'http://example.com/' with a bare slash is accepted, consistent with an optional path that begins with '/'. Each requirement maps to a distinct check.

